{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>He who seeks good obtains favor, and he who searches for evil — it shall come upon him; he who trusts in his wealth shall fall, but the righteous shall flourish like a leaf (Proverbs 11:27).</b>" "<b>King Shelomo</b>, upon him be peace, admonishes here that a person should strive for his fellow's good and not strive for his harm or injury, for a person is destined to eat the fruit of his deeds and be requited according to all that he does, measure for measure. Each and every one eats what he sows and gathers the fruit of what he plants, whether righteous or wicked — as is written of the righteous (Isaiah 3:10–11): ״אמרו צדיק כי טוב כי פרי מעלליהם יאכלו״, and it is written of the wicked (ibid.): ״אוי לרשע רע כי גמול ידיו יעשה לו״. The term שוחר is from the meaning of prayer, as in the verse (Proverbs 8:17): ״ומשחרי ימצאוני״. Therefore it says, שוחר טוב יבקש רצון — one who seeks his fellow's good and prays for him, behold, he seeks the favor of HaShem, may He be exalted. And so did our Rabbis, of blessed memory, admonish concerning this and explain to us the benefit, as they said: One who prays for his fellow and he himself needs that same thing — he is answered first, as it is said (Job 42:10): ״ה' שב את שבות איוב בהתפללו בעד רעהו״. And likewise a leader who is unique in his generation is obligated to pray on behalf of the people of his generation, and if he did not pray he is punished for it — as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded concerning the unintentional murderer who flees to cities of refuge (Numbers 35:25): ״ושב שם עד מות הכהן הגדול״ — why was the matter suspended upon the death of the Kohen Gadol? Because he should have sought mercy for the people of his generation and did not seek it; that is, he was obligated to pray on their behalf that they be deemed meritorious, so that the sin of murder would not occur in his days. And anyone who refrains from praying is called a sinner, as we find with Shemuel the prophet, upon him be peace, who said (I Samuel 12:23): ״חלילה לי מחטוא לה' מחדול להתפלל בעדכם״ — he called the withholding of prayer a sin. And so we find with David, upon him be peace, that he would pray for the creatures, for their misfortunes and for their illnesses, as he said (Psalms 35:13): ״ואני בחלותם לבושי שק עניתי בצום נפשי ותפלתי על חיקי תשוב״, and he said further (ibid. 141): ״כי עוד ותפלתי ברעותיהם״, that is, as long as I am alive I shall pray for them because of their troubles. And from what is written, ותפלתי על חיקי תשוב — 'and my prayer shall return upon my bosom' — we learn that one who prays on behalf of his fellow, the benefit returns to him, measure for measure, and this is what it says: שוחר טוב יבקש רצון. And the same applies in the reverse: if one exerts himself against his fellow and causes him harm, the harm returns to him — and this is what it says: ודורש רעה תבואנו, for it is not enough that he does not seek his welfare and good, but rather he seeks his harm; therefore shall it come upon him, measure for measure. בוטח בעשרו הוא יפול — most of those who seek evil are those who trust in their wealth, for wealth is the cause that a person's heart grows bold against people, to take vengeance and do them harm, and therefore it adjoins to it בוטח בעשרו, for the word הוא refers back to the one who trusts. And behold, this too is measure for measure: he trusts in his wealth, and therefore he shall fall because of his wealth, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 5:12): ״עושר שמור לבעליו לרעתו״. Or the word הוא refers back to wealth, for as a punishment for having trusted in his wealth, his wealth shall fall, as it is written (ibid.): ״ואבד העושר ההוא בענין רע״, and it says of wealth (Proverbs 23:5): ״התעיף עיניך בו ואיננו כי עשה יעשה לו כנפים כנשר יעוף השמים״. וכעלה צדיקים יפרחו — the swiftness of the righteous one's deliverance He likened to the sprouting of a leaf, which sprouts swiftly and precedes the fruit. And behold, this stands in contrast to בוטח בעשרו, for one who trusts in his wealth shall fall swiftly, while the righteous one who trusts in the Holy One, blessed be He, shall flourish swiftly, as it is written (Psalms 92:13): ״צדיק כתמר יפרח״. This is by way of the plain sense (peshat)." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> שוחר טוב יבקש רצון — this is Mordecai. ודורש רעה תבואנה — this is Haman. בוטח בעשרו הוא יפול — this is Korah, that is, that he lost even his wealth, as it is said: ״ואת כל הרכוש״. וכעלה צדיקים יפרחו — this is Moshe and Aharon, of whom it is written: ״והנה פרח מטה אהרן לבית לוי״. Two very wealthy men were in the world, one in Israel and one among the nations: Korah in Israel, Haman among the nations. Both heeded their wives and fell. Haman heeded his wife's counsel and fell, as it is said (Esther 5:14): ״ותאמר לו זרש אשתו וכל אוהביו יעשו עץ גבוה חמשים אמה״ — and he did so, and his design was turned back upon him. Korah heeded his wife's counsel and fell: when he came from the study hall, she said to him, 'What new law did you have today from Moshe in the study hall?' He said to her, 'He expounded for us the matter of tekhelet.' She said to him, 'What is the matter of tekhelet?' He said to her, 'Thus did Moshe expound: it was said to me from the mouth of the Almighty that you shall make for yourselves tzitzit upon the four corners of your garments, and that there shall be in it one thread of tekhelet, as it is said (Numbers 15:38): ״ונתנו על ציצית הכנף פתיל תכלת״.' She said to him, 'How he mocks you! I shall make you a tallit that is entirely tekhelet.' She arose and made him a tallit entirely of tekhelet. What did he do? He gathered two hundred and fifty heads of sanhedria, most of them from the tribe of Reuven. What did he do? He clad them all in tallitot of tekhelet; he too took a tallit of tekhelet and came to Moshe. And this is what is written." "<b>And Korah son of Yitzhar son of Kehat son of Levi took.</b> What did he take? He took his tallit and came to Moshe, and it was for this reason that the passage of tzitzit was juxtaposed above." "<b>And by way of the plain sense (peshat):</b> ויקח קרח — the meaning of ויקח is 'he held fast to his dispute,' and so is its Targum: ואתפליג ('and he split')." "<b>And the sage.</b> Rabbi Abraham explained that the verse is elliptical, and its sense is 'he took men.' Or one may say ויקח קרח דתן ואבירם ואון — 'Korah took Dathan and Abiram and On' — and according to this the ו of ודתן would be superfluous." "<b>And behold:</b> Dathan and Abiram and On, whom Korah took for himself, held fast to this dispute and followed his path and supported his enterprise and consented to it, because they thought that Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, had removed the birthright from Reuben their father and given it to Joseph; and they suspected Moses of doing so for the sake of Joshua his minister, who was of the tribe of Ephraim, and that he made for him a standard by itself. And since Korah was a firstborn, as it is written (Exodus 6:21): ״ובני יצהר קרח ונפג וזכרי״, and likewise the two hundred and fifty men, princes of the congregation, were also firstborn, and the service of the sacrifices belonged to the firstborn, including the offering of incense for many generations, therefore Korah took them into his company to argue against Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, concerning the exchange of the birthrights." "<b>And the cause</b> of Korah in this dispute was two things: the first, that he clothed himself in the trait of envy, which is an evil trait and a wound that has no cure; and the second, that he erred regarding the chain of descendants destined to come forth from him. As for the trait of envy — it is well known that when a person envies someone who has attained wealth and possessions, he piles worry upon worry until he attains the like; and if he attains it, he arrives from there to love of honor and the pursuit of kinds of dominion in acquisitions of this world; and from love of honor he ascends to rulership; and from rulership he arrives at worshipping strange gods; and if he does not attain it, all his days will be anguished and he will live a life of sorrow, and in the end he will kill either himself or his fellow. Himself — as Solomon said (Proverbs 14:30): ״ורקב עצמות קנאה״; and likewise Eliphaz said (Job 5:2): ״ופותה תמית קנאה״ — one who is enticed after the trait of envy, it kills him. And so our Sages of blessed memory expounded: envy and lust and honor remove a person from the world. His fellow — for through envy one comes to hatred, and from hatred to murder, as it is written (Deuteronomy 19:11): ״וכי יהיה איש שונא לרעהו וארב לו וקם עליו והכהו נפש ומת״. And of this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 4:4): ״וראיתי אני את כל עמל ואת כל כשרון המעשה כי היא קנאת איש מרעהו גם זה הבל ורעות רוח״ — 'all toil' refers to bodily acquisitions such as wealth, possessions, honor, and other prerogatives of rulership; 'all skill of work' refers to Torah and repentance and good deeds; 'for this is a man's envy of his fellow' — whether in wealth, in wisdom, or in any excellence whatsoever, envy is found between a man and his fellow; 'this too is vanity' — even in this sin did Hevel stumble, for he (Cain) was jealous of Hevel his brother when he brought an offering to HaShem. And he (Cain) arose and compared himself in his offering to his father, who brought a living creature; and through envy they came to hatred, and there was a quarrel between them over the inheritance of the land until he was killed. And behold, this trait is the destruction of the world. Also the ministering angels were jealous of the first man, until they caused him to sin and stripped him of the garments of knowledge, and he put on other garments — garments of traitors — until he was banished from his greatness and the Shekhinah withdrew from the lower beings. And likewise Korah's envy banished him from the world.\n\nAnd this envy of Korah — and of all those who followed his path and rallied to his obedience — was over the division of the appointments and the ranks in Israel. And they thought that Moses did this of his own mind, for from what Moses said to them, ״לא מלבי״ — 'not from my own heart' — we can infer their evil intention, for they were saying that he did it from his own heart. And they argued that his exchanging the Levites in place of the firstborn was done only to give greatness and preeminence to the sons of Levi, who are of his family — also the sons of Kohath, whose rank he elevated above all the rest of the Levites to carry the sacred objects, because they are closer to him, since Kohath was his grandfather. And as for his binding the Levites upon Aaron and his sons, the dissenters thought he did this to give greatness to Aaron and his sons. Also Elizaphan son of Uzziel — he appointed him as prince; and since Uzziel was one of the brothers of Moses' father and the youngest of them all, therefore the appointment of Elizaphan his son was grievous to Korah, and he was jealous on this account. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory expounded: What did Korah see to dispute with Moses? He was jealous of the appointment of Elizaphan son of Uzziel. Korah said: My father's brother had four brothers — Amram and Izhar and Hebron and Uzziel. Amram took the birthright, and his two sons took greatness — one a king and one a High Priest. Who is fit to take the second? Is it not I, for I am the son of Izhar, who is second to Amram? And yet he appointed the prince through the youngest of them all — I will dispute with him and nullify his words! What did he do? He gathered two hundred and fifty heads of Sanhedrin, most of them from the tribe of Reuben. What did he do? He dressed them all in cloaks of tekhelet. And this is what they said: ויקח קרח — what did he take? He took his cloak and came to Moses, and so did all of them. They said to him: A cloak that is entirely tekhelet — is it obligated in fringes? He said to them: It is obligated. They began to laugh at him. They said: Is it possible that a cloak of another material — one thread of tekhelet exempts it — but a cloak entirely of tekhelet should not all the more so? A house full of books — what is the law, is it exempt from the mezuzah? He said to them: It is obligated. They said to him: The Torah in its entirety is two hundred and seventy-five sections, and none of them exempt the house, yet the two sections in the mezuzah exempt the house?! Regarding these matters you were not commanded, and from your own heart you invent them!\n\nAnd Korah, out of his great wisdom, asked these questions, and they were of the essence of the dispute. And there is within them yet another allusion, a parable about Israel: for he compared them to a cloak entirely of tekhelet and a house full of books, and thereby intended to say that Israel — all of them are holy and all of them are distinguished, full of excellences; why do they need rulers and those who exalt themselves over them? And he compared the rulers to the thread of tekhelet and to the mezuzah.\n\nAnd the second thing — that he erred regarding the chain of descendants destined to come forth from him. As our Sages of blessed memory expounded: His eye misled him. He saw the great chain that would come forth from him — Samuel, who is weighted equal to Moses and Aaron, as it is said (Psalms 99:6): ״משה ואהרן בכהניו ושמואל בקוראי שמו״. He said: On his account I will escape. And twenty-four watches come forth from his sons and prophesy with the holy spirit from among the sons of Heman. He said: Is it possible that all this greatness shall come forth from me, and I shall be destroyed? And therefore he joined to come with force, for he heard from the mouth of Moses that all of them perish and one escapes, as it is said: ״אשר יבחר ה' הוא הקדוש״ — 'whom HaShem shall choose, he is the holy one.' He erred and attributed it to himself." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״ויקח קרח בן קהת בן לוי״ — Rabbi Levi said: Korah — who made a bald spot (קרחה) in Israel; son of Izhar — who heated (התיר) the entire world like the heat of noon (כצהרים); son of Kohath — who made two of his ancestors turn pale (קהה); son of Levi — who made a escort (לויה) for Gehenna. ודתן — who transgressed (שעבר) the law (דת) of El; ואבירם — whose heart grew stubborn (שאיבר) from doing repentance; ואון — who sat in grief (אנינות). בן פלת — in whom a wonder (פלא) was done; בן ראובן — a son who saw (ראה) and understood. קריאי מועד — who were called to the Tent of Meeting; אנשי שם — men of renown before they went forth from Egypt.\n\nAnd among those two hundred and fifty were the twelve princes who offered at the dedication of the altar. And so our Sages of blessed memory expounded from a gezerah shavah — 'called, called': it is written here ״נשיאי עדה קריאי מועד״, and it is written at Sinai in the wilderness (Numbers 1:16): ״אלה קריאי העדה נשיאי מטות אבותם״. And so Rashi of blessed memory explained: Elizur son of Shedeur and his companions — ״האנשים אשר נקבו בשמות״.\n\nAnd even though Scripture did not name them explicitly, it gave their identifying marks, and from the contexts you can recognize them. A parable — to a scion of a good family who stole vessels from the bathhouse, and the owner of the stolen goods did not wish to publicize him. He began giving identifying marks. They said to him: Who stole your vessels? He said to them: That scion of a good family — of tall stature, with fine teeth, dark hair, a handsome nose. Once he gave his identifying marks, they knew who it was. So too here — even though Scripture did not publicize them and did not specify their names, from their identifying marks you know who they are. Thus far.\n\nAnd behold, this is among the ways of the Torah, whose ways are all ways of pleasantness: at the commandment and the merit of the dedication of the altar it named them, but here, at the calamity, it concealed them.\n\nAnd it is possible to say that besides the identifying marks, there is an allusion in the names of the men. The first, who was called Elizur son of Shedeur — because from the Rock (צור) fire was cast (הושלך) upon them, as it is written: ״ואש יצאה מאת ה'״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:flags {2 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {8 "Comment [3] is marked with the Midrash method-header; comments [0]–[2] carry no explicit method-header in the Hebrew and so are rendered without one."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:52.426057105Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}